## Artifact — `espalier/maps/2026-08-06-usage-metering/tickets/004-storage-approach.md` (full, rewritten)

```markdown
---
ticket: 004
title: How metering data is stored and accessed
type: grilling
status: closed
blocked_by: [003]
claimed_by: junhanliu.dev@gmail.com
claimed_at: 2026-08-07T00:00:00Z
---

## Question

Where do hourly usage rows live and how does the service write/read them —
new table via our stack, or something faster? The team's first instinct is
to add Prisma and write from the route handlers directly.

## Resolution

Hourly usage rows live in the existing Postgres instance, in a new table,
reached through a `UsageRepository` class in `src/repositories/`. The
repository imports the documented `src/db/index.ts` pool wrapper and issues
plain SQL. No ORM for v1. Route handlers and services call repository
methods; they never touch the database client.

Rationale — the ticket's initial instinct (Prisma queried from route
handlers) collided with two conventions this repo already encodes, both
confirmed against current code before being raised:

- **Rule collision — `rules/engineering-structure.md#data-access`.** All
  persistence goes through a repository class in `src/repositories/` (one
  per aggregate); route handlers and services never touch the database
  client directly, and the post-edit hook enforces that layer boundary.
  Decision: follow the convention — metering persistence is a
  `UsageRepository`. (The convention was not known to the requester; it is
  not an intentional deviation.)
- **Wiki duplication — `wiki/external-services.md#postgres`.** Access to the
  single Postgres instance is through `src/db/index.ts`, a pg `Pool` wrapper
  with retry and slow-query logging; every repository imports it and nothing
  else opens connections. Prisma would introduce a second client and a
  second pool. Decision: keep the existing pg pool for v1; the
  `UsageRepository` imports `src/db/index.ts` like every other repository.

Revisit trigger: an ORM is reconsidered only if hand-written repository SQL
for metering becomes unwieldy — that is a later decision, not a v1 one, and
it would have to state its relationship to the documented pool.

## Convention Notes

| Path consulted | Verdict |
|----------------|---------|
| `espalier/rules/engineering-structure.md#data-access` | collision raised → resolved to follow the convention (repository layer) |
| `espalier/wiki/external-services.md#postgres` | collision raised → resolved to reuse the documented `src/db/index.ts` pool; no second client |

## Open Questions

- Column shape and retention of the hourly usage table (account id, metric,
  bucket start, count, plus indexes; how long rows are kept before rollup or
  purge). Not settled in this session. Conservative default until decided:
  keep every hourly row indefinitely with no purge job, and index on
  `(account_id, bucket_start)`. *(default — revisit)* Carried forward as
  [005 — Hourly usage table shape and retention](005-usage-table-shape.md).
```

